1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:04,282
April 1989. A baseball
movie opens in cinemas.

2
00:00:04,705 --> 00:00:08,170
It was filmed on a family farm,
near a small town in Iowa.

3
00:00:08,781 --> 00:00:12,112
Two weeks later, a stranger
pulls into the farm driveway.

4
00:00:12,567 --> 00:00:14,846
He is driving from New York to California.

5
00:00:15,255 --> 00:00:18,818
He went out of his way to see a
cornfield, before somebody plowed it up.

6
00:00:19,324 --> 00:00:20,364
He was the first.

7
00:00:21,430 --> 00:00:25,793
By late 1995, nearly 300,000
people had followed him.

8
00:00:26,207 --> 00:00:28,267
To a cornfield. In Iowa.

9
00:00:29,491 --> 00:00:33,575
Now picture your own farm. A
long road in. No highway sign.

10
00:00:33,970 --> 00:00:36,313
The nearest town, a good drive away.

11
00:00:36,800 --> 00:00:40,438
And a quiet voice that asks, "Who would
ever drive all the way out here?"

12
00:00:41,584 --> 00:00:45,289
Is your farm too far from town
for visitors? Let's find out.

13
00:00:46,118 --> 00:00:49,932
In this video, we will look at how
far farm visitors really travel.

14
00:00:50,254 --> 00:00:54,226
We will visit a real greenhouse farm,
about 60 miles from the capital,

15
00:00:54,371 --> 00:00:57,838
that welcomed 180,000
visitors in one year.

16
00:00:58,261 --> 00:01:02,210
And we will walk through three moves that
make a far-away farm worth the drive.

17
00:01:02,637 --> 00:01:06,817
Stay for move number three. It decides
whether a remote farm gets found,

18
00:01:06,988 --> 00:01:08,009
or gets forgotten.

19
00:01:08,664 --> 00:01:12,194
This channel comes from Stephen
Loke, founder of Bloopy Durians,

20
00:01:12,319 --> 00:01:16,604
a durian farm in Malaysia that welcomes
visitors from all over the world.

21
00:01:16,913 --> 00:01:18,948
And here is the part that matters for you.

22
00:01:19,278 --> 00:01:21,637
Visitors reach his farm
by four-wheel drive.

23
00:01:22,439 --> 00:01:27,142
Here is what most farmers believe.
Visitors only go to farms close to town.

24
00:01:27,489 --> 00:01:29,728
Near the highway. Near the people.

25
00:01:30,396 --> 00:01:34,334
That belief made sense for one kind
of farm. Call it the Drive-By Farm.

26
00:01:34,753 --> 00:01:37,587
A stand by the road. A hand-painted sign.

27
00:01:37,924 --> 00:01:42,114
A car slows down, someone buys a
bag of apples, and drives on.

28
00:01:42,546 --> 00:01:46,576
The Drive-By Farm lives on passing
traffic. Far from the traffic,

29
00:01:46,719 --> 00:01:47,548
it starves.

30
00:01:48,395 --> 00:01:51,808
But passing traffic is not the
only way people find a farm.

31
00:01:52,326 --> 00:01:59,257
A study of agritourism visitors in Kansas found the
average visitor travelled 129 miles to get there.

32
00:01:59,720 --> 00:02:03,139
To be fair, more than half came
from under 50 miles away.

33
00:02:03,462 --> 00:02:06,567
But the people who drove a long
way pulled that average up,

34
00:02:06,727 --> 00:02:08,038
and they came on purpose.

35
00:02:08,444 --> 00:02:09,278
In Oregon,

36
00:02:09,452 --> 00:02:17,190
a 2018 survey of agritourism farms found about a quarter of
visits came from people who travelled 50 miles or more.

37
00:02:17,604 --> 00:02:18,313
One way.

38
00:02:18,899 --> 00:02:23,262
So people do drive. The question
was never, "How far is my farm?"

39
00:02:23,667 --> 00:02:26,816
The real question is, "What's
waiting at the end of the road?"

40
00:02:28,003 --> 00:02:31,950
That is the difference between a Drive-By
Farm and a Worth-the-Drive Farm.

41
00:02:32,318 --> 00:02:36,636
One waits for traffic. The other gives
people a reason to plan the trip.

42
00:02:37,378 --> 00:02:39,071
Now, what this is not.

43
00:02:39,470 --> 00:02:44,092
This is not a promise that every
remote farm will pull 300,000 people.

44
00:02:44,401 --> 00:02:48,565
And nobody is telling you to build a
theme park, or to move closer to town.

45
00:02:48,962 --> 00:02:51,076
You can keep farming exactly as you do.

46
00:02:51,525 --> 00:02:54,051
The fix is in what people
find when they arrive,

47
00:02:54,237 --> 00:02:56,208
and how they find you
before they leave home.

48
00:02:57,050 --> 00:03:02,036
Iceland, 1995. Two young
graduates, Knútur and Helena,

49
00:03:02,193 --> 00:03:04,685
buy a run-down greenhouse
farm in a small village,

50
00:03:04,819 --> 00:03:06,695
about 60 miles from the capital.

51
00:03:07,013 --> 00:03:08,571
The farm is called Fridheimar.

52
00:03:08,980 --> 00:03:12,901
He studied agriculture. She studied
growing plants under glass.

53
00:03:13,220 --> 00:03:16,803
For years they repaired the place
and grew tomatoes all year round,

54
00:03:16,943 --> 00:03:18,983
heated by water from the hot springs.

55
00:03:19,342 --> 00:03:23,846
But a tomato is a tomato. Theirs went
out the gate like everyone else's.

56
00:03:24,458 --> 00:03:27,754
Therefore, in 2008, they opened the gate.

57
00:03:28,186 --> 00:03:31,594
Visitors could watch how they
bred horses and grew tomatoes.

58
00:03:32,017 --> 00:03:34,637
In the first year, 900 people came.

59
00:03:34,979 --> 00:03:37,378
Not a crowd. But they noticed something.

60
00:03:38,032 --> 00:03:41,283
People loved standing inside
a warm, bright greenhouse,

61
00:03:41,480 --> 00:03:43,444
while the Icelandic winter blew outside.

62
00:03:43,767 --> 00:03:48,175
So, in 2012, they put a restaurant
right in the middle of the greenhouse.

63
00:03:48,510 --> 00:03:52,402
Tables between the tomato vines.
Tomato soup with fresh bread.

64
00:03:52,791 --> 00:03:55,487
Tomato ice cream. Even tomato beer.

65
00:03:55,887 --> 00:04:00,129
Picture it. Snow outside.
You sit inside, warm,

66
00:04:00,325 --> 00:04:03,697
eating soup made from tomatoes
growing a few feet from your table.

67
00:04:04,300 --> 00:04:09,019
By 2019, they welcomed 180,000
visitors in one year.

68
00:04:09,412 --> 00:04:12,185
About 85 percent came
from outside Iceland.

69
00:04:12,586 --> 00:04:14,901
Today the farm employs dozens of people.

70
00:04:15,209 --> 00:04:18,847
Knútur once said tourism was
supposed to be their side business.

71
00:04:19,281 --> 00:04:21,436
It turned into one of
their main businesses.

72
00:04:21,953 --> 00:04:28,209
Did they move closer to the city? No.
Same farm. Same village. Same road.

73
00:04:28,825 --> 00:04:31,974
They didn't fix the distance.
They made the trip worth it.

74
00:04:33,083 --> 00:04:36,008
If you are thinking, "My farm
has to be found first,"

75
00:04:36,144 --> 00:04:38,866
Stephen wrote a step-by-step
Google guide for farms.

76
00:04:39,191 --> 00:04:42,421
The link is in the description.
More about it at the end.

77
00:04:42,729 --> 00:04:46,823
And if this is helping, subscribe,
so the next farm lesson finds you.

78
00:04:47,638 --> 00:04:51,255
Move number one. Give them one
thing they cannot get in town.

79
00:04:51,659 --> 00:04:53,862
Fridheimar had lunch inside a greenhouse.

80
00:04:54,293 --> 00:04:58,201
The Iowa farm had a walk around
the bases of a movie ball field.

81
00:04:58,593 --> 00:05:00,223
Neither one sold more stuff.

82
00:05:00,546 --> 00:05:03,025
They sold one experience
you could only have there.

83
00:05:03,462 --> 00:05:04,308
On your farm,

84
00:05:04,456 --> 00:05:08,055
that might be breakfast with eggs
the children collected themselves.

85
00:05:08,410 --> 00:05:09,966
Fruit eaten straight from the tree.

86
00:05:10,284 --> 00:05:12,221
A ride across the fields at sunset.

87
00:05:12,749 --> 00:05:13,832
Here is the test.

88
00:05:14,281 --> 00:05:17,234
If they can get it at a supermarket
ten minutes from home,

89
00:05:17,391 --> 00:05:19,512
it will not pull them
sixty miles from home.

90
00:05:20,084 --> 00:05:23,124
So finish this sentence. "Only
at our farm can you..."

91
00:05:23,439 --> 00:05:26,674
Whatever goes in that blank is
your reason for the drive.

92
00:05:27,526 --> 00:05:30,628
Move number two. Make the
road part of the adventure,

93
00:05:30,815 --> 00:05:32,878
and make the visit long
enough to be worth it.

94
00:05:33,302 --> 00:05:36,811
Remember, visitors reach Bloopy
Durians by four-wheel drive.

95
00:05:37,254 --> 00:05:43,093
A bumpy road that would scare off a supermarket shopper
becomes the first story people tell their friends.

96
00:05:43,572 --> 00:05:46,155
People will not drive an hour
for a twenty-minute stop.

97
00:05:46,477 --> 00:05:48,625
But they will drive an
hour for a half-day out.

98
00:05:49,007 --> 00:05:53,008
Something to do. Something to
eat. Something to take home.

99
00:05:53,555 --> 00:05:57,212
That is exactly how the Iceland
farm grew. A horse show.

100
00:05:57,616 --> 00:06:01,538
A walk through the greenhouse.
Lunch. A little farm shop.

101
00:06:01,863 --> 00:06:04,281
Each one gives a guest
another reason to stay,

102
00:06:04,463 --> 00:06:06,296
and another way for the farm to earn.

103
00:06:06,776 --> 00:06:10,994
And tell people the drive before they
ask. "Forty-five minutes from town,

104
00:06:11,140 --> 00:06:13,094
and the last stretch is a farm track."

105
00:06:13,428 --> 00:06:16,852
A long drive that is expected
feels like part of the trip.

106
00:06:17,274 --> 00:06:20,350
A long drive that is a surprise
feels like a mistake.

107
00:06:21,298 --> 00:06:24,766
Move number three. The one that
decides found or forgotten.

108
00:06:25,387 --> 00:06:29,678
Nobody stumbles across a remote farm.
Every visitor decides at home,

109
00:06:29,857 --> 00:06:32,600
on a phone, before the
car leaves the driveway.

110
00:06:33,078 --> 00:06:37,747
Google says its local results are based
mainly on three things. Relevance,

111
00:06:37,869 --> 00:06:39,668
distance, and prominence.

112
00:06:40,010 --> 00:06:44,301
Distance, you cannot change.
But the other two, you can.

113
00:06:44,769 --> 00:06:48,471
Relevance means how well your listing
matches what people search for.

114
00:06:48,912 --> 00:06:53,990
A complete profile, the right category,
real photos, your opening hours.

115
00:06:54,323 --> 00:06:56,192
Prominence means how well known you are.

116
00:06:56,526 --> 00:06:59,782
Google says reviews and links
from other websites play a part.

117
00:07:00,212 --> 00:07:03,332
And here is the good news for
a farm without a big budget.

118
00:07:03,662 --> 00:07:06,978
Google says there is no way to
pay for a better local ranking.

119
00:07:07,571 --> 00:07:09,759
The Drive-By Farm is found by accident.

120
00:07:10,194 --> 00:07:12,541
The Worth-the-Drive Farm
is found on purpose.

121
00:07:12,967 --> 00:07:17,895
That is the difference between a remote farm
that gets found and one that gets forgotten.

122
00:07:18,767 --> 00:07:23,492
Maybe you are thinking, "My farm is
not 60 miles out. It is hours out."

123
00:07:24,005 --> 00:07:27,094
Here is the honest answer.
The further away you are,

124
00:07:27,287 --> 00:07:28,983
the bigger your reason has to be.

125
00:07:29,500 --> 00:07:32,223
An hour out, a half-day experience works.

126
00:07:32,595 --> 00:07:37,553
Several hours out, people need
somewhere to sleep. Tents. A cabin.

127
00:07:37,973 --> 00:07:42,573
A farm stay. Then the distance stops
being a drive and becomes a getaway.

128
00:07:43,033 --> 00:07:46,308
Distance raises the bar. It
does not close the gate.

129
00:07:47,450 --> 00:07:52,308
Think back to that stranger in 1989.
He didn't care how far the farm was.

130
00:07:52,633 --> 00:07:54,421
He cared about what was waiting there.

131
00:07:55,140 --> 00:07:57,935
Watching this won't change
anything. Doing will.

132
00:07:58,670 --> 00:08:02,529
So here is your homework. In the
comments, finish the sentence,

133
00:08:02,715 --> 00:08:04,074
"Only at our farm can you..."

134
00:08:04,521 --> 00:08:06,716
and tell us how far you are
from the nearest town.

135
00:08:07,315 --> 00:08:10,340
Then, if you want to be found
before people leave home,

136
00:08:10,504 --> 00:08:12,431
get Stephen's Google guide for farms.

137
00:08:12,750 --> 00:08:15,718
It's called "Stop Being the
Best-Kept Secret in Your County."

138
00:08:16,120 --> 00:08:16,831
Inside,

139
00:08:17,028 --> 00:08:20,871
you will see the three farms Google
shows first and what wins those spots,

140
00:08:21,043 --> 00:08:24,203
a farm category guide, a
fifteen-shot photo list,

141
00:08:24,397 --> 00:08:27,310
scripts for asking for
reviews, reply templates,

142
00:08:27,500 --> 00:08:30,694
and a thirty-day plan with a
fifteen-minute weekly routine.

143
00:08:31,218 --> 00:08:33,558
It comes with a thirty-day
money-back guarantee.

144
00:08:33,890 --> 00:08:37,231
Click the link in the description, and
the guide downloads straight away.

145
00:08:37,775 --> 00:08:41,288
Next, watch "Start Agritourism
With What You Already Own."

146
00:08:41,676 --> 00:08:43,827
It shows how to open the
gate without a loan.

147
00:08:44,566 --> 00:08:47,542
Don't fix the distance.
Make it worth the drive.

